# Folder structure (strict)

Organize `src/` by responsibility. Always import via the `@/*` alias (maps to `src/*` in `tsconfig.json`) — never deep relative paths like `../../../lib/utils`.

## src/ layout

- `app/` — Next.js App Router routes, layouts, route handlers, and server actions only
- `components/`
  - `ui/` — generic, reusable primitives (shadcn-style), **kebab-case** files
  - `common/` — shared, domain-aware components used across features
  - `<feature>/` — components specific to one feature (e.g. `documents/`, `share/`)
- `sections/` — full-page compositions, one **PascalCase** folder per page
- `hooks/` — custom React hooks, one per file, `useX` naming
- `lib/` — utilities, integrations, and cross-cutting shared types
- `utils/` — small standalone helper functions
- `providers/` — React context providers and app-level setup
- `constants/` — app-wide constants
- `data/` — static fixtures / seed data
- `styles/` — global CSS

## Page composition (routes → sections)

Keep `app/` routes thin. A `page.tsx` should **only** render its top-level page component from
`sections/` — no markup or logic of its own beyond data fetching and auth guards. Each page gets a
**PascalCase** folder in `sections/` containing one main component (named after the folder) plus the
section components it composes.

```
src/
├── app/
│   └── documents/page.tsx    # only: auth guard + render <DocumentsPage />
└── sections/
    └── DocumentsPage/
        ├── DocumentsPage.tsx # main page component, composes the sections below
        ├── OwnedSection.tsx
        ├── SharedSection.tsx
        └── ...
```

```tsx
// src/app/documents/page.tsx
import DocumentsPage from "@/sections/DocumentsPage/DocumentsPage"

const Page = () => <DocumentsPage />

export default Page
```

- The main page component (`DocumentsPage.tsx`) is the single component referenced by `page.tsx`.
- Section components reused across pages belong in `components/common/`; section components specific
  to one page stay inside that page's `sections/<PageName>/` folder.

## Naming

| Kind                  | Convention              | Example                     |
| --------------------- | ----------------------- | --------------------------- |
| React components      | PascalCase `.tsx`       | `DocumentCard.tsx`          |
| UI primitives (`ui/`) | kebab-case              | `button.tsx`, `dialog.tsx`  |
| Hooks                 | `use` + PascalCase      | `useAutosave.ts`            |
| Utilities / helpers   | camelCase               | `formatRelativeTime.ts`     |
| Props types           | `*Props` suffix         | `type DocumentCardProps = { ... }` |

## One component per file (strict)

Every `.tsx` file must contain **exactly one** React component. Never declare two or more components
in the same file.

- If a component is built from sub-components (cards, rows, list items, toolbars, dialogs, etc.),
  extract **each** sub-component into its own `.tsx` file and import it.
- Group a section's sub-components in a subfolder beside it, e.g.
  `sections/DocumentEditor/editor/EditorToolbar.tsx`.
- Non-component code is exempt and should live in non-`.tsx` files (or co-located): types, constants,
  static data, custom hooks, helper/utility functions, and `cva`/variant definitions.
- The only component in the file should be the one the file is named after.

```
// ❌ Bad — DocumentEditor.tsx defines EditorToolbar, ToolbarButton, ShareDialog, … in one file
// ✅ Good
sections/DocumentEditor/
├── DocumentEditor.tsx        # one component: <DocumentEditor /> (composes the pieces)
└── editor/
    ├── EditorToolbar.tsx     # one component
    ├── ToolbarButton.tsx     # one component
    └── editorExtensions.ts   # non-component: Tiptap extension config
```

## Rules

- **One component per file** (see above) — this is non-negotiable.
- **Named exports** for reusable components/utilities; **default export** only for page-level routes and `sections/` compositions.
- Co-locate component-specific types inline near the component; put cross-cutting/shared types in `lib/`.
- Group new code into the directory that matches its responsibility — do not pile feature logic into `app/`.
